<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colin Renfrew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Andrew Colin Renfrew, Baron Renfrew of Kaimsthorn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colin Renfrew, Baron Renfrew of Kaimsthorn, was a British archaeologist, paleolinguist and Conservative peer noted for his work on radiocarbon dating, the prehistory of languages, archaeogenetics, neuroarchaeology and the prevention of looting. In Before Civilisation (1973) he challenged the assumption that prehistoric cultural innovation originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His Anatolian hypothesis, set out in Archaeology and Language (1987), held that Proto-Indo-European originated about 9,000 years ago in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and spread with farming, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis. He was Disney Professor of Archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> from 1981 to 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colin-renfrew.jpg" alt="Colin Renfrew" width="330" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Portable Antiquities Scheme from London, England · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TVC_June_2018_(29165787578)_(Renfrew_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>David Anthony regards the Pontic–Caspian steppe as the strongest candidate for the homeland of Proto-Indo-European, with the possible exception of the Anatolian languages, while <b>Colin Renfrew</b> has argued for a Near Eastern origin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Colin Renfrew</b> argued in 1973 that the great monuments of Neolithic Wessex, Stonehenge among them, were raised by labour that chiefs mobilised from wide territories, and the mounds and plazas of Cahokia were built in the same way.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
